message.reasoning_content:
The user wants a translation of a C program to PHP.
The C program reads integers from standard input until EOF.
For each integer, it checks if it's odd or even.
If odd, prints "Ehab".
If even, prints "Mahmoud".

In PHP, reading from stdin until EOF is typically done with `fgets(STDIN)` in a loop or `stream_get_contents` or iterating over `STDIN`.
Since the C code uses `scanf("%d", &n)` in a loop, it processes whitespace-separated integers.
PHP's `fgets` reads line by line. A line might contain multiple numbers.
However, typical competitive programming problems (this looks like Codeforces 959A) usually have one integer per line or just one integer total.
The C code `while (scanf("%d",&n) != EOF)` handles multiple integers separated by any whitespace.

To replicate this exactly in PHP:
Read all input from STDIN, split by whitespace, iterate over tokens.

Code structure:
<?php
$stdin = fopen('php://stdin', 'r');
while (($line = fgets($stdin)) !== false) {
    $numbers = preg_split('/\s+/', trim($line));
    foreach ($numbers as $num_str) {
        if ($num_str === '') continue;
        $n = (int)$num_str;
        if ($n % 2 != 0) {
            echo "Ehab\n";
        } else {
            echo "Mahmoud\n";
        }
    }
}
fclose($stdin);
?>

Alternatively, simpler for typical online judges where input is small:
$data = file_get_contents('php://stdin');
$tokens = preg_split('/\s+/', trim($data));
foreach ($tokens as $token) {
    if ($token === '') continue;
    $n = (int)$token;
    if ($n % 2 != 0) echo "Ehab\n";
    else echo "Mahmoud\n";
}

The prompt asks for *only* the translated code, no extra description.

